{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NSBRLzaCBpB4TSLuS1zp8W","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## 2026-09-01 — WI-572: the approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk\n2\t\n3\t**Spec of record:** `../plans/2026-09-01-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`\n4\t(the owner's ruling, 2026-09-01, recorded in\n5\t`../log.md` from `2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md`). Serialized behind\n6\tWI-571 (the copy-scope row); both touch `intake.py` / `baseline_snapshot.py`.\n7\t\n8\t**In one line:** a worker lane may author `Drafted` spine rows and amend cell\n9\ttext, but the approval act — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded` and\n10\tthe `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is the adjudicator's,\n11\tperformed on the serial trunk side.\n12\t\n13\t### The baseline this row is measured against\n14\t\n15\tEvery commit that moved an `\"Approved\"` string in a spine registry before the\n16\truling, classified by where it happened:\n17\t\n18\t- **1 worker-lane flip** — `580df781` (WI-508 slice 6), whose next review round\n19\t  returned CHANGES-REQUESTED against exactly those flips.\n20\t- **4 lanes minted rows born `Approved`**, skipping the brief entirely —\n21\t  `8848f6fb` (WI-483), `ad2222df` (WI-500), `69e4a854` (WI-501), `0cfb2e6f`\n22\t  (WI-507).\n23\t- The rest were trunk sittings or the pre-ladder rename.\n24\t\n25\tfig: 17 commits, classified by subject; `git log --format='%h|%s'\n26\t-S'\"Approved\"' fd86e47f -- docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\n27\tdocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml docs/test/test-cases.toml\n28\tdocs/requirements/system-requirements.csv\n29\tdocs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv docs/test/test-cases.csv`\n30\tat `fd86e47f`.\n31\t\n32\t### Deliverables\n33\t\n34\t**1. A lane's merge is refused when its delta performs an approval act.**\n35\t`acceptance_record.staged_approval_acts` reports a `Status` crossing INTO\n36\t`Approved`/`Founded` and a row that arrives already claiming one;\n37\t`lane_approval_refusal` words the refusal, naming each row, its registry, the\n38\tshape of act, and every `docs/archive/last_approved/` file the branch touched —\n39\teach worded by what the branch DID to it (round 4; see below).\n40\t`integrate._approval_act_refusal` is the rung, beside `_minted_id_refusal`\n41\twhose shape it copies exactly — the merge base, and the ladder placement.\n42\t\n43\tConstruction-first, as the plan required: `staged_spine_amendments` already\n44\tdiffs the merged commit and EXEMPTS a row whose Status moved; the new reader\n45\treports that exempted set MINUS the de-approvals (round 4). All three readers (plus\n46\t`staged_drafted_rows`) now share ONE two-tree walk, `_spine_row_sides`,\n47\textracted rather than copied. Verified against the record itself: the reader\n48\treproduces the census above exactly — four flips at `580df781`, and the\n49\tborn-`Approved` rows of all four lanes.\n50\t\n51\tThe judgement lives in `acceptance_record`, not in the merge slot, on LLR-178's\n52\tseparation: the coordinator that merges is not the reader that decides what a\n53\tspine delta did. That also kept `integrate.py`'s size bump to a new rung's\n54\tirreducible core.\n55\t\n56\t**2. The first-approval adjudication arm.** If a lane may not approve what it\n57\tauthored, something must. `intake._first_approval_drafts` (trigger a2) mints ONE\n58\t`brief = \"first-approval\"` adjudication per merge over the `Drafted` rows the\n59\tdelta added or amended — the exact mirror of trigger (a), one section below it.\n60\t`agent_common.SPINE_APPROVAL_RUNGS` names the DevStg rung each spine tier is\n61\tapproved into so the dial can answer whether that tier is loop-held; a rung the\n62\tdial HOLDS is not minted (the owner approves those, through the approval brief,\n63\tas today), and an unmapped tier is held. (That table was `intake.APPROVAL_RUNG`\n64\tuntil REVIEW-A; see \"The dial filter the brief did not have\" below.)\n65\t\n66\t`prompts/adjudicate-first-approval.template.md` is the fifth adjudicator brief,\n67\twith `adjudicate_brief.first_approval_values` behind it and its own verdict\n68\tgrammar (`OUTCOME: APPROVE|RETURN rows=N`). The brief renders each row's WHOLE\n69\tCHAIN, which is the owner's stated reason the act is the adjudicator's — so\n70\t`trace.reattest_model`'s `chain_of` closure became the public\n71\t`trace.spine_chain` + `chain_buckets`. The model's own `rows` list carries only\n72\twhat changed or is `Drafted`: the right answer to the re-attest brief's question\n73\tand exactly the wrong one here, where the settled parent and the passing sibling\n74\ttest ARE the evidence.\n75\t\n76\t`integrate._adjudication_lane` exempts the actor the ruling names. The\n77\tconcurrency half the owner asked for was **already built** and is not\n78\tre-implemented: an adjudication row is not `ordinary`, so\n79\t`dispatch._branch_exclusive` already runs its lane alone. The ruling is what\n80\tpoints the act at that guarantee.\n81\t\n82\t**3. The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The template's closing\n83\t\"the flip, if one is owed, is the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" went false\n84\twhen OI-45 (b) retired that tool, so a MEANING verdict on a loop-held rung ended\n85\tat a brief nobody was owed. Replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot\n86\t(`adjudicate_brief._aftermath`), which reads the declared gate authority for the\n87\ttiers actually shown and tells the session whether the re-attestation is its own\n88\tact or the owner's — rather than leaving it to read and interpret a dial\n89\tmid-verdict. `prompts/CATALOG.md` regenerated.\n90\t\n91\t**4. The doctrine says it once.** PROCESS.md §4 gains one clause in its fixed\n92\tpoints and links to PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the\n93\truling, its two reasons, the division-of-labour table the owner asked for in\n94\t§2a, and the three holding mechanisms). OI-45 (b) gains the narrowing sentence.\n95\t`gate-advance` names the acceptor its procedure was already addressed to;\n96\t`spine-authoring` opens with \"authoring is not approving\"; `worker.template.md`\n97\tgains the NEVER clause and its close ritual now says \"minted or amended\". The\n98\ttwo reference surfaces follow the mechanism they describe:\n99\t`docs/enforcement-audit.md` gains the rule's row (with its three-fold honest\n100\tresidue), and `docs/registry-machinery-reference.md` records the other half of\n101\tthe amendment walk, plus the narrowing on the `Founded` row's OI-45 citation.\n102\t\n103\t**5. Tests**, all in the modules' existing style: five at the merge slot, four\n104\tat the reader, four at the trigger, and — counting the terminal-sequence\n105\tregression of round 3 and the three scope regressions of round 4 — nine at the\n106\tbrief, plus the widened schema pin and the loader triplet's third member.\n107\t\n108\t**6. The two registry rows this lane's own code moved.** New code under an\n109\texisting row's Module leaves that row's cells stale, and the rule from the\n110\tWI-553 reading is that a lane re-points its own staleness IN-LANE rather than\n111\tdeferring it:\n112\t\n113\t- `LLR-158` (`Approved`) — its `code_symbol` named four symbols; the module now\n114\t  carries the shared two-tree walk and three more readers off it, and the row's\n115\t  `Detail` gained the sentence that explains them. Amended, **not flipped**:\n116\t  there is no status for a post-approval amendment, the row stays `Approved`,\n117\t  and the DRIFT against `docs/archive/last_approved/` is the signal.\n118\t- `IF-091` (`Drafted`) — `integrate` is now a second requestor of the seam, so\n119\t  it is named as one. Without it the new import is a cross-component crossing\n120\t  that no interface row declares.\n121\t\n122\tBoth warns were **introduced by this branch** and both are gone;\n123\t`check_trajectory` exits 0 with only the pre-existing `schedule -> trace`\n124\tcrossing standing. The amendment was checked against this row's OWN mechanism\n125\trather than assumed safe: `staged_approval_acts` returns `[]` and\n126\t`lane_approval_refusal` returns `None` over `4d0b972d..<staged tree>` — amending\n127\tis not approving — while `staged_spine_amendments` reports `LLR-158`, so the\n128\tamendment adjudication trigger (a) raises a row for it at merge. This lane's own\n129\tedit is the first customer of the arm the same lane shipped, which is the\n130\tintended shape and not a special case.\n131\t\n132\tRE-CHECKED AT THE FINAL TIP, because the reading above was taken at a staged\n133\ttree BEFORE the close, and the close changes an input this rung reads: draining\n134\t`docs/work/active/` empties `_claimed_specs`, so `_adjudication_lane` answers\n135\tFalse and this branch is judged as the work lane it is, with no exemption. Over\n136\t`4d0b972d..78e20e4e` the answers are unchanged — `staged_approval_acts` `[]`,\n137\t`lane_approval_refusal` `None`, `staged_spine_amendments` still reporting\n138\t`LLR-158`. The branch that added the refusal is merge-clean under it at the tree\n139\tthe integrator sees, which is the only tree the claim is worth anything at.\n140\t\n141\tThe amend-without-flip guard then fired on `LLR-158`, both arms, and both are\n142\tanswered rather than absorbed:\n143\t\n144\t- **The drift arm** — \"re-attest in this commit, or the change rides as SNAPSHOT\n145\t  DRIFT until the next sitting\". Riding as drift is not a miss here, it is the\n146\t  ruling: the lane may not re-attest its own amendment, and `intake.py snapshot`\n147\t  on this branch is precisely what deliverable 1 refuses. The drift IS the\n148\t  handover.\n149\t- **The `Hat-Refs` arm** — it says an unchanged cell cannot distinguish \"re-read\n150\t  and unchanged\" from \"never re-read\", so the answer belongs in the record.\n151\t  DELIBERATE: the cell stays inherited (`LLR-158` declares none and takes\n152\t  SR-178's `TEST-ENGINEER`). The amendment documents more of the SAME mechanism\n153\t  — one walk, four readers — and does not move the row's question, which is\n154\t  still how an amendment is DETECTED. Who may act on what is detected is a\n155\t  governance concern; it lives on the PROCESS surfaces and in `integrate`'s\n156\t  rung, not in this row's cells. Re-read, unchanged, and said so.\n157\t\n158\t### REVIEW-A rework: the dial filter the brief did not have\n159\t\n160\tRound 1 returned CHANGES-REQUESTED with one MAJOR finding, and it was right.\n161\t\n162\t**What was wrong.** `intake._released_drafted_rows` filters the minted\n163\tpopulation by the dial — a rung the owner still holds is not handed to an\n164\tadjudicator. `adjudicate_brief.first_approval_values` then RE-RESOLVES that\n165\tpopulation live at composition time, deliberately (a row minted at a merge is\n166\tclaimed later, and `red_tc_values`' rule says brief the world the judge is\n167\tactually in). But it re-resolved it from `trace.reattest_model`, which is\n168\tdial-blind by design, and never put the filter back. Re-computing live had\n169\tquietly become re-computing a WIDER question than the mint asked.\n170\t\n171\tThe consequence is not cosmetic. Under a mixed dial — `human_approval_through =\n172\t\"DevStg-Reqs\"` holds the SR tier and releases the LLR tier below it — the brief\n173\trendered a held `Drafted` SR beside a released `Drafted` LLR, marked BOTH\n174\t`[AWAITING FIRST APPROVAL]`, and derived a `--approves` argument naming both\n175\tregistries. That is a generated prompt instructing an adjudicator to perform a\n176\tsignature the owner owes, with the act's own recorded scope carrying it. At the\n177\tkit's shipped default dial (`DevStg-Release`, which holds every rung) every row\n178\tin the brief was the owner's.\n179\t\n180\t**Why the test suite did not catch it.** `_first_approval_repo` declared no\n181\t`docs/process.toml` at all, so its dial fell back to `DevStg-Release` — the\n182\tfixture was not this arm's scenario, it was the owner's, and it went green. The\n183\tfixture now declares `DevStg-Needs` explicitly, which is the honest statement of\n184\twhat the arm requires to exist.\n185\t\n186\t**The fix is a deletion, not a guard.** The rung table existed TWICE for one\n187\tcommit: `intake.APPROVAL_RUNG` keyed by registry (the mint) and\n188\t`adjudicate_brief._APPROVAL_RUNG_OF` keyed by tier (the amendment aftermath) —\n189\tand the first-approval brief, the third consumer, was wired to neither. Both\n190
